arousal modules"]}, {"t": "small", "c": ["Scoped educational mechanism"]}]}]}]}, {"t": "section", "a": {"id": "medicines", "aria-labelledby": "heading-medicines"}, "c": [{"t": "h2", "a": {"id": "heading-medicines"}, "c": ["Medicine handling and targets"]}, {"t": "div", "a": {"class": "atlas-tiles"}, "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"class": "atlas-tile", "href": "/science/process-atlas/f24/"}, "c": [{"t": "img", "a": {"src": "/assets/atlas/web/F24_medication-absorption-metabolism-and-elimination.webp", "width": "1600", "height": "1080", "loading": "lazy", "alt": ""}}, {"t": "span", "c": ["Medication absorption metabolism and elimination"]}, {"t": "small", "c": ["Scoped educational mechanism"]}]}, {"t": "a", "a": {"class": "atlas-tile", "href": "/science/process-atlas/f25/"}, "c": [{"t": "img", "a": {"src": "/assets/atlas/web/F25_medication-target-overlays.webp", "width": "1600", "height": "1080", "loading": "lazy", "alt": ""}}, {"t": "span", "c": ["Medication target overlays"]}, {"t": "small", "c": ["Scoped educational mechanism"]}]}, {"t": "a", "a": {"class": "atlas-tile", "href": "/science/process-atlas/f40/"}, "c": [{"t": "img", "a": {"src": "/assets/atlas/web/F40_amphetamine-dexamfetamine-at-the-dopamine-terminal.webp", "width": "1600", "height": "1080", "loading": "lazy", "alt": ""}}, {"t": "span", "c": ["Amphetamine dexamfetamine at the dopamine terminal"]}, {"t": "small", "c": ["Scoped educational mechanism"]}]}]}]}, {"t": "section", "a": {"aria-label": "Featured diagrams"}, "c": [{"t": "h2", "c": ["Four starting points"]}, {"t": "figure", "a": {"class": "atlas-figure", "id": "atlas-F00"}, "c": [{"t": "h3", "c": ["Whole system overview"]}, {"t": "p", "a": {"class": "atlas-scope"}, "c": ["An organising framework"]}, {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/svg/F00_whole-system-overview.svg"}, "c": [{"t": "img", "a": {"src": "/assets/atlas/web/F00_whole-system-overview.webp", "width": "1600", "height": "900", "loading": "lazy", "alt": "Whole system overview. Read from genes and regulation through development, precursor supply, cellular energy, synaptic communication and brain circuits to cognitive performance."}}]}, {"t": "figcaption", "c": ["How do biology and context connect to cognitive function? This is an organising model, not one ADHD formula or a diagnostic graph.", {"t": "span", "a": {"class": "atlas-credit"}, "c": ["MJB ADHD Process Atlas · Reviewed 2026-10-13"]}]}, {"t": "p", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/svg/F00_whole-system-overview.svg"}, "c": ["Open larger diagram"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/full/F00_whole-system-overview.png"}, "c": ["Full-size PNG"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/science/process-atlas/f00/"}, "c": ["Diagram page"]}]}, {"t": "details", "a": {"class": "atlas-text"}, "c": [{"t": "summary", "c": ["Read the diagram explanation and sources"]}, {"t": "p", "c": ["Read from genes and regulation through development, precursor supply, cellular energy, synaptic communication and brain circuits to cognitive performance. Sleep, stress and environment are additional inputs. The connecting arrows organise relationships for learning; they do not demonstrate one causal chain, one defect shared by everybody with ADHD or a diagnostic test."]}, {"t": "ul", "c": [{"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41588-022-01285-8", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Demontis and colleagues 2023 ADHD GWAS"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09702-8", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Demontis and colleagues rare variants 2026 issue"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41386-022-01408-z", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["ADHD default mode mega analysis 2023"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09702-8", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Rare genetic variants confer a high risk of ADHD and implicate neuronal biology (2025 online; 2026 issue)"]}]}]}]}]}, {"t": "figure", "a": {"class": "atlas-figure", "id": "atlas-F04"}, "c": [{"t": "h3", "c": ["Vesicle loading and storage"]}, {"t": "p", "a": {"class": "atlas-scope"}, "c": ["Established transport mechanism"]}, {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/svg/F04_vesicle-loading-and-storage.svg"}, "c": [{"t": "img", "a": {"src": "/assets/atlas/web/F04_vesicle-loading-and-storage.webp", "width": "1600", "height": "900", "loading": "lazy", "alt": "Vesicle loading and storage. ATP hydrolysis powers the vesicular proton pump, V-ATPase, which acidifies the vesicle."}}]}, {"t": "figcaption", "c": ["ATP powers the vesicle's proton pump. VMAT2 uses the resulting proton gradient to load monoamines for storage and later release; it is not directly powered by ATP. This normal mechanism does not establish an individual transport defect.", {"t": "span", "a": {"class": "atlas-credit"}, "c": ["MJB ADHD Process Atlas · Reviewed 2026-10-13"]}]}, {"t": "p", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/svg/F04_vesicle-loading-and-storage.svg"}, "c": ["Open larger diagram"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/full/F04_vesicle-loading-and-storage.png"}, "c": ["Full-size PNG"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/science/process-atlas/f04/"}, "c": ["Diagram page"]}]}, {"t": "details", "a": {"class": "atlas-text"}, "c": [{"t": "summary", "c": ["Read the diagram explanation and sources"]}, {"t": "p", "c": ["ATP hydrolysis powers the vesicular proton pump, V-ATPase, which acidifies the vesicle. VMAT2 exchanges vesicular protons for cytosolic monoamines such as dopamine. It therefore uses an existing proton gradient rather than directly hydrolysing ATP itself. Stored transmitter can subsequently be released by exocytosis. This explains a normal mechanism, not evidence of a VMAT2 defect in a particular reader."]}, {"t": "ul", "c": [{"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06926-4", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Human VMAT2 transport and inhibition 2024"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41422-023-00906-z", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["VMAT2 vesicle loading 2024"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10770148/", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Transport and inhibition mechanism for VMAT2-mediated synaptic vesicle loading of monoamines — full text"]}]}]}]}]}, {"t": "figure", "a": {"class": "atlas-figure", "id": "atlas-F36"}, "c": [{"t": "h3", "c": ["Mitochondrial membrane and respiratory chain"]}, {"t": "p", "a": {"class": "atlas-scope"}, "c": ["Established cellular mechanism"]}, {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/svg/F36_mitochondrial-membrane-and-respiratory-chain.svg"}, "c": [{"t": "img", "a": {"src": "/assets/atlas/web/F36_mitochondrial-membrane-and-respiratory-chain.webp", "width": "1600", "height": "900", "loading": "lazy", "alt": "Mitochondrial membrane and respiratory chain. NADH supplies electrons through complex I, while succinate oxidation supplies electrons through complex II."}}]}, {"t": "figcaption", "c": ["Electrons pass along the respiratory chain while complexes I, III and IV pump protons. Proton return through ATP synthase supports ATP formation. This illustrates general cell physiology; it does not establish an energy defect in a reader.", {"t": "span", "a": {"class": "atlas-credit"}, "c": ["MJB ADHD Process Atlas · Reviewed 2026-10-13"]}]}, {"t": "p", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/svg/F36_mitochondrial-membrane-and-respiratory-chain.svg"}, "c": ["Open larger diagram"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/full/F36_mitochondrial-membrane-and-respiratory-chain.png"}, "c": ["Full-size PNG"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/science/process-atlas/f36/"}, "c": ["Diagram page"]}]}, {"t": "details", "a": {"class": "atlas-text"}, "c": [{"t": "summary", "c": ["Read the diagram explanation and sources"]}, {"t": "p", "c": ["NADH supplies electrons through complex I, while succinate oxidation supplies electrons through complex II. Ubiquinone transfers electrons to complex III, cytochrome c carries them to complex IV, and oxygen is the final acceptor. Complexes I, III and IV pump protons from the matrix towards the intermembrane space. Complex II is membrane-bound but does not pump protons; its label is placed on the matrix side in this simplified drawing. Proton return through ATP synthase supports ATP formation from ADP and phosphate. The diagram does not establish mitochondrial dysfunction in ADHD."]}, {"t": "ul", "c": [{"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://reactome.org/content/detail/R-HSA-611105", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Reactome respiratory electron transport"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://reactome.org/content/detail/R-HSA-163210", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Reactome ATP formation"]}]}]}]}]}, {"t": "figure", "a": {"class": "atlas-figure", "id": "atlas-F39"}, "c": [{"t": "h3", "c": ["Common locus to effector gene evidence"]}, {"t": "p", "a": {"class": "atlas-scope"}, "c": ["Evidence interpretation framework"]}, {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/svg/F39_common-locus-to-effector-gene-evidence.svg"}, "c": [{"t": "img", "a": {"src": "/assets/atlas/web/F39_common-locus-to-effector-gene-evidence.webp", "width": "1600", "height": "900", "loading": "lazy", "alt": "Common locus to effector gene evidence. Begin with an associated locus, then evaluate fine-mapping, candidate variants, regulatory or coding consequences, potential effector genes and tissue-specific functions."}}]}, {"t": "figcaption", "c": ["A genetic association identifies a region of interest. Connecting it to a candidate variant, gene and tissue-specific process requires several kinds of evidence. Each step has uncertainty; proximity alone does not establish causality.", {"t": "span", "a": {"class": "atlas-credit"}, "c": ["MJB ADHD Process Atlas · Reviewed 2026-10-13"]}]}, {"t": "p", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/svg/F39_common-locus-to-effector-gene-evidence.svg"}, "c": ["Open larger diagram"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/atlas/full/F39_common-locus-to-effector-gene-evidence.png"}, "c": ["Full-size PNG"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/science/process-atlas/f39/"}, "c": ["Diagram page"]}]}, {"t": "details", "a": {"class": "atlas-text"}, "c": [{"t": "summary", "c": ["Read the diagram explanation and sources"]}, {"t": "p", "c": ["Begin with an associated locus, then evaluate fine-mapping, candidate variants, regulatory or coding consequences, potential effector genes and tissue-specific functions. Each step needs its own evidence. Linkage disequilibrium can leave several correlated candidates, and regulation may act over a distance. Proximity can be a clue but does not establish the causal gene by itself. These are complementary evidence types, not a mandatory sequence or a validated causal conclusion about one person."]}, {"t": "ul", "c": [{"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41588-022-01285-8", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Demontis and colleagues 2023 ADHD GWAS"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41588-025-02295-y", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Childhood ADHD symptoms and diagnosis GWAS 2025"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41588-025-02383-z", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["2025 GWAS publisher correction"]}]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41588-022-01087-y", "rel": "noopener"}, "c": ["Combining SNP-to-gene linking strategies to identify disease genes and assess disease omnigenicity"]}]}]}]}]}]}, {"t": "section", "a": {"aria-label": "A useful visual guide"}, "c": [{"t": "figure", "a": {"class": "atlas-figure", "id": "read-the-evidence"}, "c": [{"t": "h3", "c": ["Three different evidence questions"]}, {"t": "img", "a": {"loading": "lazy", "src": "/assets/visual-guides/read-the-evidence.svg", "width": "1440", "height": "850", "alt": "Three different evidence questions. A mechanism, an association and an individual conclusion are different kinds of evidence. A connection between these questions needs evidence; it is not automatic."}}, {"t": "figcaption", "c": ["Use the question to recognise the scope of a claim. A normal pathway does not prove a defect; an association does not prove an individual cause."]}, {"t": "p", "c": [{"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/science/process-atlas/"}, "c": ["Read the related explanation"]}, " · ", {"t": "a", "a": {"href": "/assets/visual-guides/read-the-evidence.svg"}, "c": ["Open full-size illustration"]}]}, {"t": "details", "c": [{"t": "summary", "c": ["Read the image as text"]}, {"t": "p", "c": ["A mechanism, an association and an individual conclusion are different kinds of evidence."]}, {"t": "ul", "c": [{"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "strong", "c": ["Mechanism"]}, ": How can a biological process work? Enzymes, transporters, cells and model experiments."]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "strong", "c": ["Association"]}, ": What varies together in a study? A statistical result needs its sample, effect and uncertainty."]}, {"t": "li", "c": [{"t": "strong", "c": ["Individual conclusion"]}, ": What does this mean for this person? Clinical and genetic interpretation needs additional evidence."]}]}, {"t": "p", "c": ["A connection between these questions needs evidence; it is not automatic."]}]}]}]}]}}